\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Partial Grassmann integration and the Schur complement}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{V=R\oplus E} be a direct sum of finite-dimensional
complex vector spaces, with inclusions #{i_R,i_E} and
projections #{p_R,p_E}. For #{K\in\operatorname{End}(V)} define
the compressions
##{\begin{gathered}
A=p_RKi_R,\quad B=p_RKi_E,\\
C=p_EKi_R,\quad D=p_EKi_E,
\end{gathered}}
and, when #{D} is invertible, #{S=A-BD^{-1}C}. No adjoint
relation between #{B} and #{C} is assumed. Let #{J_E} be the
retained-first paired coefficient of \ref{fdip-00IX}, and let
#{J_R} be the intrinsic paired coefficient on the retained
summand. Then #{J_RJ_E=J_V}. The eliminated top degree
#{2\dim E} is even, so moving that top element past a retained
coefficient gives no further sign. With zero sources,
##{J_E\left(\ue^{-\bar\psi(K\psi)}\right)
 =\det_E D\,\ue^{-\bar\psi_R(S\psi_R)},}
and consequently
##{\det_V K=\det_E D\,\det_R S.}
Empty summands use determinant one and the identity coefficient
map. Invertibility of #{K} or #{S} is unnecessary. The map
#{D} is a compression; it is a restriction into #{E} only when
#{K(E)\subseteq E}. \ref{fdip-00IX} restores sources in this
identity.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{\psi_R=p_R\psi}, #{\bar\psi_R=\bar\psi\,i_R}, and
likewise for #{E}. Complete the eliminated quadratic by
##{\begin{aligned}
\chi_E&=\psi_E+D^{-1}C\psi_R,\\
\bar\chi_E&=\bar\psi_E+\bar\psi_RBD^{-1}.
\end{aligned}}
The bars remain labels of independent generators. Expanding
in this multiplication order gives
##{\bar\psi(K\psi)
 =\bar\psi_R(S\psi_R)+\bar\chi_E(D\chi_E).}
The last term of #{\bar\chi_E(D\chi_E)} is
#{\bar\psi_R(BD^{-1}C\psi_R)}, which cancels the subtraction
in #{S}. All these quadratic elements are even. They commute,
so their finite exponentials multiply.}

\p{Choose an ordered basis of #{E} and use the paired
generators of that basis, with every retained generator
standing to the left. Replacing an eliminated generator by
itself plus a retained odd linear combination preserves its
partial top coefficient. Expand each exterior monomial in the
new symbols: every term that uses a retained replacement has
fewer eliminated generators, while the term that uses no
replacement has coefficient one and the original order.
Terms of lower eliminated degree cannot contribute to the
top degree. Thus the coefficient in the shifted quadratic
exponential equals the coefficient for independent
#{E}-generators. The [paired determinant identity](fdip-008H)
evaluates it as #{\det_E D}. The retained exponential factors
on the left, proving the partial formula. Applying #{J_R}
and the same determinant identity proves the factorization.}
}

\p{The scalar here is #{\det_E D}. The real positive Gaussian
fiber integral of \ref{fdip-000D}, for an eliminated real
block of the same size #{q=\dim E}, instead has the factor
#{(2\pi)^{q/2}(\det D)^{-1/2}} under its real positive
hypotheses and volume convention. Neither positivity nor an
ordinary integration measure was used in the Grassmann proof.}

\p{Remark. The partial coefficient #{J_E} reads the coefficient
of a volume element of the top exterior power of the eliminated
block. The factorization
#{\det_V K=\det_E D\,\det_R S}
is the induced factorization of that top exterior power, so
the retained factor is the exterior-algebra complement of the
eliminated block.}

\transclude{fdip-00MG}
